# Configuration for the couchbase-fleetmanager service.
#
# FM_ARGS is appended to the server's command line by systemd. Note this is not a shell
# script: no command substitution, no ${VAR} expansion, one VAR=value per line. The server
# reads no environment variables of its own, so FM_ARGS is the only setting with any effect.
#
# After editing:  systemctl restart couchbase-fleetmanager
#
# Full documentation: /usr/share/doc/couchbase-fleetmanager/README.md
# All flags:          /opt/couchbase/fleetmanager/bin/fleetmanager-server --help
#
# Common flags (--ui-dir and --credentials-path are already set by the unit file):
#
#   --connection-string couchbase://host1,host2   default: couchbase://localhost
#   --bucket-name <name>                          default: default
#   --scope-name <name>                           default: fleetmanager
#   --port <n>                                    default: 443 (80 with --insecure)
#   --cert <path> --key <path>                    both or neither; self-signed if omitted
#   --insecure                                    plain HTTP, for use behind a TLS proxy
#   --admin-user <name>                           initial UI admin, first startup only
#   --log-level debug|info|warn|error             default: info
#
# Two things that catch people out:
#   - couchbases:// validates against the host trust store, so the cluster CA must be
#     installed there; a cluster's own self-signed certificate will be rejected.
#   - --cert and --key must be readable by the fleetmanager user, and the service runs with
#     ProtectHome=yes, so paths under /root or /home are invisible to it.
#
# This file holds no secrets and cannot usefully hold any, since everything in FM_ARGS
# becomes a command-line argument visible via /proc. Couchbase credentials live in
# /opt/couchbase/var/lib/fleetmanager/credentials.json instead.
FM_ARGS=
